clothing_type,detergent,fabric_softener,temperature,spin,duration,program,options,ironing,ironing_notes,iron_setting,drying,colour_group,mix_tags,notes
White,"Universal powder, full dose — the bleach in powder is what keeps whites from going grey",no,60,1200,~2:30,Cottons,Eco,yes,"Iron while slightly damp, steam on. Cotton takes all the heat the iron has.",3,"Tumble dry normal, or line dry in the sun — sunlight bleaches whites for free.",white,,Wash whites on their own. One stray coloured sock is all it takes.
White Socks,"Universal powder, full dose",no,60,1200,~2:30,Cottons,Eco,no,,,Tumble dry normal.,white,,Same settings as White — throw them in that load.
White Towels,"Universal powder, full dose. Go lighter rather than heavier: excess detergent stiffens terry.",no,60,1400,~2:30,Cottons,Extra Rinse,no,Ironing crushes the pile flat and the towel stops drying you.,,Tumble dry medium — the tumbling is what fluffs the pile back up.,white,lint-shedder,Never fabric softener: it coats the loops in silicone and the towel stops absorbing. White vinegar in the softener drawer once a month softens instead.
Coloured,"Colour liquid detergent — no bleach, no optical brighteners",yes,40,1200,~2:15,Cottons,Eco,yes,"Steam, but turn prints and dark panels inside out first.",3,"Line dry inside out, or tumble on low.",colour,,Wash new or strongly dyed items separately for the first two or three washes.
Coloured Socks,Colour liquid detergent,no,40,1200,~2:15,Cottons,Eco,no,,,Tumble dry low or line dry.,colour,,Same settings as Coloured — throw them in that load.
Coloured Towels,Colour liquid detergent. Go lighter rather than heavier: excess detergent stiffens terry.,no,40,1400,~2:15,Cottons,Extra Rinse,no,Ironing crushes the pile flat and the towel stops drying you.,,Tumble dry medium — the tumbling is what fluffs the pile back up.,colour,lint-shedder,Never fabric softener — it kills absorbency. Run them at 60 °C every month or so if they start to smell.
Microfibre Towels,"Liquid detergent, half dose. What doesn't rinse away stays in the split fibre and stops it picking anything up.",no,40,1200,~1:30,Easy Care,Extra Rinse,no,"Polyester and polyamide start melting around 110 °C, well before the iron does anything useful.",,"Air dry, or tumble on low. High heat fuses the split ends shut and the towel never absorbs again.",colour,lint-magnet,Never fabric softener — it fills the split fibres with silicone and the towel is finished. Wash with microfibre only: the same split ends that make it absorbent grab cotton lint and never let go.
Dark,Liquid detergent for dark/black — optical brighteners are what leave that grey haze,no,30,800,~2:00,Cottons,Extra Rinse,yes,"Inside out, medium heat. Steam, don't press hard — shine on dark cotton doesn't come out.",2,"Line dry inside out, out of direct sun. Skip the dryer: heat fades black fastest.",dark,dye-bleeder,Turn everything inside out. The extra rinse matters here — detergent residue dries as grey streaks on black.
Black Socks,Liquid detergent for dark/black,no,30,800,~2:00,Cottons,Extra Rinse,no,,,"Line dry, or tumble on low.",dark,,Same settings as Dark — throw them in that load.
Denim,Liquid detergent for dark/black,no,30,800,~2:00,Cottons,Extra Rinse,yes,Inside out on cotton heat if you want them crisp. Steam.,3,Hang by the waistband and air dry. A hot dryer shrinks them and destroys the elastane in stretch denim.,dark,dye-bleeder,"Inside out, zips and buttons closed. Wash them far less often than you think."
Raw Denim,"A capful of pH-neutral or denim-specific detergent. No bleach, no enzymes.",no,cold,400,~0:45,Delicates / Silk,,no,Pressing sets creases in the wrong places and flattens the fades that took months to earn.,,"Hang inside out indoors, away from sun and radiators. Never tumble.",dark,solo|dye-bleeder,"Unwashed denim is meant to go months before its first wash. Gentler than the machine: a 45-minute cold soak in the bath, inside out, no agitation, no wringing — then roll in a towel to press the water out."
Merino Wool,"Wool detergent, half dose. No enzymes, no proteases, no bleach — they eat the fibre.",no,30,400,~0:40,Wool,,yes,"Only if you must: pressing cloth, no weight. Hanging it in a steamy bathroom drops the creases without touching it.",2,"Never tumble dry. Dry flat on a towel, away from radiators and sun — hung up it stretches and never comes back.",any,,"Turn inside out, wash with wool only, nothing with zips. Merino barely needs washing — air it overnight and wear it again."
Synthetic Activewear,A small dose of mild pH-neutral liquid detergent. Too much is what makes them smell.,no,30,800,~1:10,Sportswear,Extra Rinse,no,"Elastane starts degrading around 110 °C, and the technical finish goes with it.",,Air dry on a hanger — dry within the hour. Tumble heat kills the stretch.,sport,lint-magnet,"Inside out. Never softener: it films over the fibres these rely on for wicking and stretch. A cup of white vinegar in the rinse resets a load that already smells. Covers tops, shorts and base layers — one fabric family, one wash. Mixed colours are fine in one run: at 30 °C the dyes on polyester barely move. The exception is a brand-new black or strong red piece, which can crock for a wash or two — give that its own run first."
Gym Socks,"Liquid detergent, normal dose",no,40,1200,~1:20,Sportswear,Extra Rinse,no,,,Tumble dry low or line dry.,sport,lint-shedder,Give them a 60 °C cotton run every few weeks — 40 °C keeps them clean but doesn't reset the smell.
Cashmere Blend,"Wool or cashmere detergent, half dose. No enzymes, no proteases, no bleach.",no,30,400,~0:40,Wool,,yes,"Low heat through a pressing cloth. Better still, steam it on a hanger and let the creases drop out on their own.",2,"Never tumble dry. Squeeze the water out, never wring, then dry flat on a towel away from any heat.",any,,"Inside out, and only ever with other wool. Cashmere pills where it rubs — that is the fibre behaving normally, not a fault; a cashmere comb takes it off. Check the blend first: a little nylon makes it tougher, silk makes it more delicate."
Wool Trainers,A small amount of mild detergent — a wool detergent is ideal. No bleach.,no,cold,400,~0:40,Wool,,no,,,"Air dry only, somewhere with moving air: outside, by a window, or in front of a fan. Never the dryer and never a radiator — heat warps the sole and shrinks the wool upper.",any,solo,"Pull the insoles and laces out first and wash those by hand. The shoes go in a mesh delicates bag or a pillowcase, on their own — a loose trainer batters the drum and everything in it."
